I"5<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>Generative AI and foundation models are moving fast. In radiology, one practical question is whether they could be good assistants for spotting mistakes in reports by cross-checking them against the X-ray.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>We created 1,000 reports with synthetic errors from two real-world chest X-ray datasets. We then set two kinds of tasks: a yes-or-no question, whether there is an error, and a multi-way question, what type of error it is. Multimodal language models were tested on both.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>
<p>Among publicly available models, LLaVA v1.5 did best at the yes-or-no check. Instruction tuning, which means training it on examples of this task, improved it by 47.4% on MIMIC-CXR and 25.4% on IU X-ray, and it surpassed the domain expert’s accuracy on MIMIC-CXR by 1.67%. Where a clinician got a case wrong in a small test set, the ensemble of models picked up 71.4% of those. All the models did poorly at naming the type of error, so that harder task remains open.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>Reports with errors can delay or misdirect care. An AI second reader that checks the words against the image could be a useful safety net, which is why we kept developing this line of work.</p>
